  "response": "## The finding\n\n`crime_00068` — **Duplicate Component Shape**, `severity: medium`, `confidence: 0.7`, shape hash `e96b55b64336`, 4 files / 4 occurrences:\n\n| File | Lines |\n|---|---|\n| `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` | 142–150 |\n| `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` | 152–160 |\n| `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` | 101–109 |\n| `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` | 112–120 |\n\nReading the four sites, the shape is the **form footer / action bar**: a `div.mt-6.flex.justify-end.gap-4` wrapping a `Link` to `/dashboard/invoices` labelled \"Cancel\" and a `<Button type=\"submit\">`. Across all four copies exactly one token varies — the submit label (`Create Invoice` vs `Edit Invoice`). The wrapper classes, the href, the cancel label, and the cancel link's 6-class style string are byte-identical.\n\nThat's the drift risk: a change to the footer (a `disabled` state while the action is pending, a focus ring, a `gap` tweak, a destructive \"Delete\" button) has to be hand-copied into four places, and nothing fails if you copy it into three.\n\n## One caveat before the refactor\n\n`final-example` and `starter-example` are **two separate Next.js apps** (before/after stages of the same tutorial), each with its own `app/`, its own `@/` alias, and its own copy of `ui/button.tsx`, `ui/skeletons.tsx`, etc. — that's why 40-odd `exact_duplicate_block` findings pair the two. Collapsing across that boundary would break the apps' independence.\n\nSo: **one shared component per app, two files created, each consumed by two call sites.** The cross-app half of the finding is expected and should stay.\n\n## Proposed component\n\n`app/ui/invoices/form-actions.tsx` (created identically in each app):\n\n```tsx\nimport Link from 'next/link';\nimport { Button } from '@/app/ui/button';\nimport type { ReactNode } from 'react';\n\nexport function FormActions({\n  submitLabel,\n  cancelHref = '/dashboard/invoices',\n  cancelLabel = 'Cancel',\n  children,\n}: {\n  submitLabel: string;\n  cancelHref?: string;\n  cancelLabel?: string;\n  children?: ReactNode;\n}) {\n  return (\n    <div className=\"mt-6 flex justify-end gap-4\">\n      <Link\n        href={cancelHref}\n        className=\"flex h-10 items-center rounded-lg bg-gray-100 px-4 text-sm font-medium text-gray-600 transition-colors hover:bg-gray-200\"\n      >\n        {cancelLabel}\n      </Link>\n      {children}\n      <Button type=\"submit\">{submitLabel}</Button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n### Public API\n\n**Props**\n- `submitLabel: string` — **required**, the only genuinely varying value today. Required rather than defaulted, so a new form can't silently inherit \"Create Invoice\".\n- `cancelHref?: string` — defaults to `/dashboard/invoices`. All four sites use the default; the prop exists for a future customer or settings form.\n- `cancelLabel?: string` — defaults to `'Cancel'`.\n\n**Slots**\n- `children?: ReactNode` — secondary actions, rendered between Cancel and Submit (the natural place for a future \"Delete invoice\" or \"Save as draft\"). One slot is enough; splitting into `leading`/`trailing` invites layout decisions to leak back into callers.\n\n**Deliberately not in the API**\n- No `className` / `style` escape hatch. The whole point is that the container's layout classes live in one file; a `className` prop lets each form re-diverge and the detector stops seeing it.\n- No `submit?: ReactNode` slot. Passing the whole button back in reintroduces the duplication with extra steps.\n- No `pending` prop yet. When you wire disabled-while-submitting, put `useFormStatus()` inside `Button` — that keeps the state out of this component's surface and out of all four callers. It's worth naming because it's the concrete edit that would currently need four hand-copies.\n\nNo `'use client'` directive needed on this file — it holds no hooks or handlers, and it's imported by client components either way.\n\n## Files that change\n\n**Created (2)**\n- `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/form-actions.tsx`\n- `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/form-actions.tsx`\n\n**Edited (4)** — each replaces its 9-line `<div>` with `<FormActions submitLabel=\"…\" />` and swaps the now-unused `Link` import (the `Button` import goes too in all four; `Link` is unused afterwards in all four — check `create-form.tsx` in each app, where the footer is `Link`'s only use):\n\n| File | Replaces | Becomes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` | 142–150 | `<FormActions submitLabel=\"Create Invoice\" />` |\n| `dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` | 152–160 | `<FormActions submitLabel=\"Edit Invoice\" />` |\n| `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx` | 101–109 | `<FormActions submitLabel=\"Create Invoice\" />` |\n| `dashboard/starter-example/app/ui/invoices/edit-form.tsx` | 112–120 | `<FormActions submitLabel=\"Edit Invoice\" />` |\n\nNet: −36 lines of JSX, +2 files, 4 imports rewritten. `effort: small` matches.\n\n## What this does and doesn't clear\n\nRe-running `crimes scan --format json` should drop the finding with fingerprint `duplicate_component_shape::dashboard/final-example/app/ui/invoices/create-form.tsx::::e96b55b64336`. That's the verification.\n\nIt will **not** clear the neighbouring findings on the same four files, and they're worth knowing about because two of them touch the very same subtrees:\n\n- `crime_00018` / `crime_00019` — `\"paid\"` / `\"pending\"` compared in 4 files across 2 expression shapes (`invoice.status === \"paid\"` in the edit forms, `status === \"paid\"` in `status.tsx`). Different fix: a policy helper, not a component.\n- `crime_00074` / `00075` / `00078` / `00079` — Design Token Escape, the `h-[18px] w-[18px]` and `px-[14px]` literals. Those live in the *upper* part of each form (the icon and the status fieldset), not in the footer, so this extraction doesn't touch them.\n- The status fieldset itself (lines 88–134 / 96–144 in the final-example pair) is a much larger near-duplicate that the shape detector didn't group — the two copies differ in the `defaultChecked` props and one `className`. That's the higher-value second extraction (`InvoiceStatusFieldset`), but it needs the `paid`/`pending` policy decision resolved first, so it's a separate change.\n\nI haven't edited anything — these files are `crimes` scanner input under `evals/fixtures/`, so changing them would move the eval baseline. Say the word if you want the refactor applied somewhere other than the fixture.",
